    "narrative": "The primary objective for Ghost Ride the WHIP today is maximizing our speed-reliant scoring floor, making **Mickey Moniak** (75.75) our standout play. Facing Gusto, **Mickey Moniak** benefits from a massive 14% park-factor boost and a deliberate delivery from the mound, which significantly elevates his stolen base probability\u2014a critical metric in our 5x5 framework. **Josh Naylor** (65.22) follows as our second-strongest option; despite a slight park penalty, his strong dynamic platoon split against Ure\u00f1a and his high-leverage spot in the fifth hole make him a reliable cornerstone. While **Kyle Karros** (56.78) also draws the favorable Gusto matchup, his lower order position suppresses his ceiling compared to the top-tier options.\n\nOur optimization process was most contentious at the fringe, where **Zach McKinstry** (36.27) fell just short of our 40-point start threshold. Although **Zach McKinstry** typically offers speed utility, Eovaldi\u2019s sub-3.50 xERA and a poor platoon outlook rendered him a liability today. We chose to bench **Zach McKinstry** to preserve our game caps, favoring the safer, albeit marginal, value of **Travis Bazzana** (45.98). While **Travis Bazzana** has a difficult matchup against Martin, his leadoff spot provides essential volume that we simply couldn't ignore given the scarcity of plate appearances in this league.\n\nManagement must remain vigilant regarding our back-end starters, as **Dalton Rushing** (56.35) and **Austin Riley** (51.91) are both facing elite-hold pitchers capable of suppressing stolen base opportunities. **Dalton Rushing** is particularly tethered to the 8-hole, limiting his run-producing potential. We are holding **Corbin Carroll** and **Vinnie Pasquantino** in the reserves due to an off-day and IL designation, respectively, which keeps our bench depth thin for the remainder of the week. With **Brice Turang** (47.43) facing a high-skill arm in Burns, we are relying on our core mid-range scorers to maintain efficiency while avoiding the deeper risks inherent in the bottom of the order.",
